digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56842-6
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Investigate Au-Pd fivefold icosahedral multiply twinned nanoparticles by using atomic electron tomography to determine three-dimensional atomic coordinates and quantify misfit, strain, and defect/disorder distributions, corroborating the analysis with molecular-dynamics simulations to test and develop a strain-relief model in which intrinsic fivefold angular deficit is accommodated by localized defects, amorphization, and Janus-like structural heterogeneity.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When an important nanoparticle class has unresolved internal strain mechanisms, limitations of projection-based imaging, and source-grounded evidence that defects or disorder can participate in strain accommodation, the appropriate research aim is to determine 3D atomic structure and evaluate a mechanistic strain-relief model with quantitative 3D evidence."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Inhomogeneous strain distributions observed together with dislocations and disclinations support the more limited inference that localized defect-related mechanisms are plausible contributors to strain accommodation in fivefold multiply twinned particles."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Reported property advantages in icosahedral or fivefold twinned metal nanoparticles motivate investigating how fivefold structure, strain, and atomic arrangement relate to nanoparticle function."];
  "R17" [label="(0,0,0) Because 3D local strain and internal defect structures in fivefold icosahedral MTPs remain unclear while AET can support quantitative 3D misfit and strain measurements, an AET-based study should determine 3D atomic coordinates and quantify defect/disorder and strain distributions to build or validate a strain-relaxation model."];
  "n1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently there exist multiply twinned particles (MTPs) where tetrahedra twin to form decahedra or icosahedra with fivefold symmetry (i.e., fivefold twinning is common)."];
  "n11" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, there exist different competing strain models (homogeneous and inhomogeneous) proposed to explain internal distortion and space-filling."];
  "n12" [label="(9,1,0) Currently, the homogeneous strain model implies simple linear displacement throughout the lattice."];
  "n13" [label="(10,1,0) Currently, inhomogeneous strain distributions have been observed with evidence of dislocations."];
  "n14" [label="(10,2,0) Currently, inhomogeneous strain distributions have been observed with evidence of disclinations."];
  "n16" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, final crystallographic symmetry is determined by a subtle balance between surface and volume contributions to total energy (in addition to internal strain)."];
  "n18" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, elastic strain energy from atomic distortion can be compensated by reducing surface energy through optimal surface atomic arrangements."];
  "n19" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, Ino and Marks proposed adding extra crystal planes to classical decahedron models to reduce surface energy."];
  "n2" [label="(2,1,0) Currently, fivefold twinned nanoparticle structures receive sustained attention across multiple fields (e.g., crystal growth)."];
  "n22" [label="(15,1,0) Currently, these surface-energy-reduction models were confirmed in core-shell decahedral MTPs where extra high-index planes at corners/edges further reduce surface energy."];
  "n23" [label="(16,1,0) Currently, during growth of icosahedra, faulted islands are observed."];
  "n26" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, faulted islands and grooves form curved surfaces that lower surface energy and relieve strain."];
  "n27" [label="(18,4,0) Currently, the 3D atomic structures of icosahedra and their associated local strain remain long-standing problems."];
  "n28" [label="(18,7,0) Currently, embedded internal structures (e.g., dislocation/disclination distributions) remain unclear due to complexity and limitations of conventional projection imaging."];
  "n29" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, high-resolution electron microscopy images from fivefold directions are commonly used to elucidate MTP atomic structures (but they are projection-based)."];
  "n3" [label="(3,2,0) Currently, for icosahedral Pt alloy catalysts, superior ORR activity may arise from strain-induced electronic effects (so strain matters for function)."];
  "n31" [label="(21,2,0) Currently, fivefold diamond microcrystallite boundaries contain coherent twins with occasional dislocations to accommodate misfit."];
  "n32" [label="(22,1,0) Currently, oriented attachment of small crystal domains can induce fivefold twins (as proposed via in situ electron microscopy)."];
  "n33" [label="(23,2,0) Currently, although observations are predominantly based on 2D projections, the internal structures were not obtained."];
  "n34" [label="(24,1,0) If a method can visualize internal atomic structures in 3D at atomic resolution, then it can address limitations of projection imaging for complex MTPs."];
  "n35" [label="(24,0,0) Currently, atomic resolution electron tomography (AET) advances offer a method to visualize internal atomic structures of nanomaterials in 3D (and even 4D)."];
  "n36" [label="(25,1,0) Currently, AET observed 3D core structures of edge dislocations releasing strain in a Pt decahedral MTP."];
  "n38" [label="(27,2,0) Currently, quantitative measurements of 3D inherent atomic misfit and strain in fivefold MTPs were not obtained, precluding thorough understanding."];
  "n39" [label="(0,0,0) If the 3D atomic structure and local strain state are unclear and quantitative 3D strain/misfit is missing, then a study should determine 3D atomic coordinates and quantify 3D misfit/strain and defect/disorder distributions to build/validate a strain-relaxation model."];
  "n4" [label="(4,1,0) Currently, Au icosahedral particles show more efficient SERS than similar-sized spherical particles (so fivefold/icosahedral structures can improve properties)."];
  "n40" [label="(28,0,0) Currently, the study determines 3D atomic coordinates of Au-Pd MTPs and shows 3D angular deficiency in fivefold icosahedral nanoparticles is compensated by edge dislocations and amorphization of several tetrahedra."];
  "n41" [label="(33,0,0) Currently, a strain model is unveiled: some tetrahedra on one side transform to disordered structures to release intrinsic strain, while the other side remains nearly ideal fcc."];
  "n44" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given full atomic structures/3D internal defects are required but not accessible by 2D projection methods and AET enables 3D atomic-coordinate determination, therefore use AET (with quantification) to resolve 3D structure, defects, and strain in fivefold icosahedral nanoparticles."];
  "n47" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given fivefold twinning is common in MTPs and fivefold twinned nanoparticles attract sustained attention across fields, therefore fivefold MTPs are an important and broadly relevant class of structures to study."];
  "n48" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given fcc geometry enables fivefold twinning and internal distortion is required to fill geometric gaps, therefore understanding strain/distortion mechanisms is central to explaining fivefold MTP formation and stability."];
  "n49" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given balancing surface and volume energy contributions matters and surface restructuring can compensate elastic strain via reduced surface energy, therefore surface-energy-driven relaxation mechanisms are important alongside internal strain in MTPs."];
  "n5" [label="(5,3,0) If distinct properties of MTPs are due to chemical sites and lattice-misfit strain, then knowing their full atomic structures is essential."];
  "n50" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given Ino/Marks surface-energy reduction models and later confirmations in core-shell decahedral MTPs, therefore surface-facet/plane modifications are validated strain/energy-relief strategies in fivefold MTPs."];
  "n51" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given faulted islands and grooves are observed during icosahedra growth and these features form curved surfaces that lower surface energy and relieve strain, therefore curved-surface motifs are another observed strain-relief pathway in fivefold MTPs."];
  "n52" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given fivefold-direction high-resolution microscopy is commonly used and observations are predominantly 2D projections where internal structures were not obtained, therefore projection-based imaging is insufficient to recover full internal structures of complex fivefold MTPs."];
  "n53" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given competing homogeneous and inhomogeneous strain models exist and the homogeneous model implies simple linear displacement, therefore model discrimination requires internal-structure/strain evidence beyond simple assumptions."];
  "n54" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given fivefold diamond microcrystallites show coherent twins with occasional dislocations and oriented attachment can induce fivefold twins, therefore dislocations and assembly mechanisms are recurring motifs relevant to fivefold twinning and misfit accommodation."];
  "n55" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given AET can visualize internal structures in 3D and prior AET studies show dislocations/defects in MTPs, therefore it is plausible and feasible to apply AET to quantitatively map 3D defects/strain in fivefold MTPs."];
  "n56" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given AET yields 3D internal-structure information and quantitative 3D misfit/strain is missing, therefore AET studies should include quantitative 3D misfit/strain measurements rather than qualitative defect descriptions."];
  "n57" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given specific study observations (angular deficiency compensated by dislocations/amorphization) and the unveiled Janus-like strain model with MD corroboration, therefore a Janus-like hemispheric strain-compensation model is supported as a concrete target for validation."];
  "n58" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given fivefold/icosahedral structures can improve properties and MTP properties are linked to strain/chemical sites, therefore resolving 3D structure and strain is necessary for understanding structure–property relationships in fivefold MTPs."];
  "n6" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, MTPs have distinct physical/chemical properties largely due to chemical sites and associated lattice-misfit strain."];
  "n7" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, fcc crystals have coplanar twin planes at ~70.53° and can form fivefold twinned decahedra/icosahedra."];
  "n9" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, to fill space gaps between adjacent (111) faces in fcc tetrahedra (~70.53°), internal distortion must be incorporated."];
  "n1" -> "n47" [label="induction-case"];
  "n2" -> "n47" [label="induction-common"];
